course: everyday
lesson: 467
title: Friends through games and clubs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Lily joins Brownies knowing nobody. First activity: build a tower out of dry spaghetti and marshmallows with a partner, Grace-from-Brownies (not Grace-from-school). Their tower is the worst in the hall. It collapses onto the leader's shoe. They laugh so much they get told off. Friends. That's what clubs do: they give strangers something to do together, and the friendship grows out of the doing. «Join something you love,» Lily's mum says, «and you'll find people who love it too.» Lily rolls her eyes, signs up for the museum dinosaur club, and finds three kids who argue passionately about whether T. rex had feathers.
explain: Mia starts a Pokémon card club at lunch with a sign she drew herself. Five kids come, including Eli, a boy nobody ever talks to. Eli turns out to know every card's value, how rare it is and which ones are fakes. Everyone starts asking him; he smiles more in a week than all year. At the chess tournament Dev from another school beats Sam on time in the final. In the corridor Dev says, «That was mad close. Wanna play online sometime?» With their dads' OK they play every week. A year later the rematch is a draw, and they high-five over the board.
explain: Jake joins boxing «to get hard, innit», expecting the big lads to batter him. Instead they show him how to wrap his hands and say «keep your guard up, little man». The coach has one rule: «What happens in the ring stays in the ring. Fight outside and you're out.» Jake gets into fewer fights at school, not more. Chloe's first drama club, a girl with a ponytail says loudly «Ugh, newbie» and rolls her eyes. Chloe nearly walks out — but stays, gets cast as a talking toilet in the panto and makes the whole club cry laughing. Next week the ponytail girl asks her to sit with her.
explain: Connor plays Fortnite every week with his cousin's mates — loud and funny on voice chat. At his cousin's birthday they meet for real and nobody knows what to say; they stand round the trampoline staring at their trainers until someone mentions the new map, and everyone talks at once. On Kieran's school Minecraft server they build a whole city with a giant statue of their teacher. «It's giving New York, fr,» says one girl — it looks like New York, for real. They nearly fall out over who builds the tallest tower, so they vote; Kieran loses and builds the best bridge instead. At football camp Connor is paired with Mateo, who only speaks Spanish. They pass for an hour; by Friday they swap «gracias» and «cheers, mate». In Perth Maddie's netball team chants «GEOR-DIE!» when she scores — a nickname that's a hug in words.
fact: Clubs and teams give people something to do together, and friendships often grow from that.
fact: Joining something you love is a good way to find people who love the same thing.
fact: Starting a club can help everyone find friends, especially quiet children who are rarely asked.
fact: Rivals in a game can become friends; shake hands whoever wins.
fact: Good sports clubs teach respect and have rules against fighting outside the sport.
fact: Don't let one unkind person make you quit something you enjoy.
fact: Shared games need shared rules: voting and taking turns settle most arguments.
fact: People you only know online can feel like strangers face to face at first; start with what you share.
fact: Play online only with people your family knows about, and with your parents' permission.
fact: Games and sport can make friends across languages, even with only a few words in common.
fact: A friendly team nickname can welcome someone in, as long as it is kind.
fact: «It's giving» means «it looks like» or «it has the feel of», and «fr» means «for real».
exercise: How did Lily and Grace-from-Brownies become friends? | Their spaghetti tower collapsed and they laughed together | They won a prize for the tallest and strongest spaghetti tower | They went to the same school | Their mums were friends
exercise: Why do clubs help people make friends? | They give strangers something to do together | Everyone there has to be your friend, it's a rule | You get a badge for each friend | The leaders pick your friends
exercise: What did Lily's mum advise? | Join something you love to meet people who love it too | Join the biggest club in town, since more people means more friends | Only join clubs your friends join | Never join clubs alone
exercise: Who did Mia's Pokémon club help most? | Eli, a boy nobody used to talk to | Mia's teacher | The popular kids who already had loads of mates | Mia's little brother
exercise: Why did everyone start asking Eli questions? | He knew every card's value and spotted fakes | He had the most cards and the rarest shiny ones too | He was the loudest | He gave cards away
exercise: What did Dev say after beating Sam? | «That was mad close. Wanna play online sometime?» | «Easy win, you're rubbish, go back to your own school» | «Never play me again» | «I want a trophy for this»
exercise: What did Sam and Dev need before playing online every week? | Their dads' permission | A new chess board | A secret password from school | Nothing at all
exercise: What did Jake expect at boxing club? | To get battered by the big lads | To get a free cake | To be the best one there on the very first day | To be bored
exercise: What was the boxing coach's rule? | Fight outside the ring and you're out | Win every fight in the ring or you have to leave | Big lads always go first | Only fight on Mondays
exercise: How did boxing change Jake at school? | He got into fewer fights | He got into more fights | He stopped going to school | He became the bully
exercise: The ponytail girl said «Ugh, newbie». What did Chloe do? | Stayed, and made the club laugh as a talking toilet | Walked out of the hall and never came back to the club | Said something meaner back | Told her mum to complain
exercise: Connor's Fortnite mates stood around awkwardly at the party. What got them talking? | Someone mentioned the new map | Someone fell off the trampoline | A grown-up made them introduce themselves | They all went home early, one by one
exercise: How did the Minecraft group settle who builds the tallest tower? | They voted | They had a fight | The loudest won | They deleted the server
exercise: «It's giving New York, fr.» What does that mean? | It looks like New York, for real | It's sending a gift to New York | It's for rent in New York | It's freezing in New York
exercise: Connor and Mateo don't share a language. How did they become friends? | By passing a football and showing tricks | By looking up every single word in a dictionary all day | They didn't, they gave up | By texting each other
exercise: The netball team chants «GEOR-DIE!» at Maddie. Is the nickname mean? | No, it's friendly and welcomes her | Yes, it's an insult about where she's from | Yes, it means «go home» | No, it's her real name
test: «drama club's full of snobs, one girl rolled her eyes, i'm quitting». What does Chloe's story suggest? | Don't let one unkind person make you quit | Quit straight away and find a club with no snobs in it | Roll your eyes back harder | Only join clubs with friends in
test: Connor meets his online mates for real and everyone goes silent. What helps? | Talk about the game they all share | Leave the party at once | Pretend he's never played | Stare at his trainers all day
test: «boxing makes you more violent, everyone knows». What happened with Jake? | He got into fewer fights thanks to the rules | He started fighting everyone at school who looked at him | He was banned from school | He beat up the coach
test: A kid online you've never met says «add me, ur proper good, wanna play every night?» What's the safe move? | Check with your parents first | Add him straight away | Give him your address | Keep it secret from your family
test: «eli's a weirdo, nobody talks to him». After the club, what did people find out? | He knew loads about cards and was great to talk to | He really was a weirdo, just like everyone had always said | He didn't like Pokémon | He left the club
test: In a film, the rival team captain is evil and stays an enemy forever. What happened with Sam and Dev? | They became friends and played weekly | They became sworn enemies | They never met again | Dev cheated in every game
test: The Minecraft kids are about to fall out over the tallest tower. What would most likely help? | A vote and taking turns | Deleting each other's buildings | Quitting the server for good | The loudest person deciding
test: Mateo speaks Spanish, Connor English. A coach says «sorry, swap partners». Was swapping needed? | No, football let them be friends without words | Yes, you can't be friends without a language | Yes, they'd fight | No, but they should text in French
test: Lily's mum: «join something you love». What happened when Lily did? | She found kids who loved dinosaurs too | She was bored stiff and never went back | She hated the museum and wanted to go home | She joined five clubs at once and was exhausted
test: The team chants a nickname at you every time you score. How can you tell it's friendly? | It's said with cheering and you're part of the team | It's shouted really loudly from the other side of the pitch | It rhymes | It's said by grown-ups
exam: Identify the principal reason for which clubs and teams facilitate the formation of friendships. | They furnish strangers with a common activity | They oblige every member to befriend every other | They award badges for each friend acquired | Their leaders allocate friends to new members
exam: Given that a pupil wishes to play an online game regularly with a person met at a tournament, ascertain the appropriate precondition. | The permission of the pupil's parents | The purchase of superior equipment | A password issued by the school | No precondition whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of a well-regulated boxing club upon the conduct of a pupil at school? | It may reduce the pupil's involvement in fights | It invariably renders the pupil more violent | It obliges the pupil to fight his classmates | It has no relation to conduct at all
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning disputes among players constructing a shared online city. | They may be resolved by voting and taking turns | They may be resolved only by deleting others' work | They require the departure of all players at once | They are invariably decided by the loudest player
